$schema: https://usm.dev/schema/v1.json
$id: usm/internal-dsl-builder
$type: feature
$version: 1
$last_updated: "2026-08-13"
summary: >-
  Fluent typed TypeScript builder (internal DSL, per Fowler) that compiles to validated .usm YAML —
  defineFeature/defineService chains populate the semantic model and build() enforces the schema,
  exported from the package's public API.
$system: usm/system
$service: usm/cli
intent: >-
  Authoring flows/contracts/tests in raw YAML is verbose and error-prone for humans and agents
  alike. Fowler's answer for this exact pain is an internal DSL: a fluent host-language builder that
  populates the semantic model and inherits the host's tooling (types, autocomplete, refactoring).
  LLMs write TypeScript far better than any bespoke grammar, so the builder aligns human ergonomics
  with agent capability. Constraints stay enforced by schema validation at build() — the YAML/JSON
  path remains first-class.
status: built
flows:
  - id: build-feature
    name: Compose a feature spec with the builder
    steps:
      - id: s1
        action: call
        target: defineFeature('org/slug', { system, service })
      - id: s2
        action: chain
        target: >-
          .summary().intent().status().flow(id, fn).contract(id, fn).test(id, fn).decision(id,
          fn).seeAlso([])
      - id: s3
        action: build
        target: returns { yaml, object, valid, errors } — schema-validated before hand-back
  - id: emit-and-write
    name: Write a built spec to disk
    steps:
      - id: s1
        action: build
        target: the spec via builder
      - id: s2
        action: write
        target: writeFeature(result, path) — refuses invalid specs, atomic write
  - id: extend-existing
    name: Extend an existing spec
    steps:
      - id: s1
        action: load
        target: defineFeature from a parsed existing .usm file
      - id: s2
        action: mutate
        target: chain additions (flows append by id)
      - id: s3
        action: rebuild
        target: revalidate
contracts:
  - id: builder-emits-valid-usm
    description: Every build() result passes schema validation or reports errors explicitly
    must_have:
      - build() runs validateUsm and includes errors in the result
      - writeFeature refuses to write when valid is false
      - Generated YAML matches MCP write-tool serialization conventions
  - id: required-fields-enforced
    description: The builder surface mirrors the schema's required fields
    must_have:
      - summary and intent required before a valid build
      - $system and $service required at construction
      - flows get steps; contracts get must_have; tests get expect
  - id: exported-public-api
    description: The DSL ships as part of the package's public entry
    must_have:
      - import { defineFeature, defineService, writeFeature } from '@smithgray/usm' works
      - Types exported for IDE autocomplete
  - id: roundtrip-fidelity
    description: Builder output parses back to the same object
    must_have:
      - yaml → parseUsm → deep-equals object
tests:
  - id: builds-valid-feature
    setup: {}
    expect:
      - assertion: "chained builder builds with valid: true"
      - assertion: yaml parses back deep-equal to object
  - id: invalid-build-reported
    setup:
      missing_intent: true
    expect:
      - assertion: "valid: false with schema errors listed"
      - assertion: writeFeature refuses and writes nothing
  - id: extend-existing
    setup:
      existing_parsed_feature: true
    expect:
      - assertion: loaded builder appends flows by id without dropping existing
      - assertion: rebuild validates
  - id: service-builder
    setup: {}
    expect:
      - assertion: defineService builds a schema-valid service with type and runtime
decisions:
  - id: internal-over-external
    decision: Internal (host-language) DSL, not a custom grammar
    rationale: >-
      Fowler's cost test: inherit TS tooling and LLM fluency, skip parser maintenance forever. The
      semantic model (.usm) remains the asset.
  - id: runtime-schema-validation
    decision: Enforce constraints by validating at build() rather than type-level branding
    rationale: >-
      Keeps the fluent API simple and the JSON Schema the single source of truth; builders cannot
      silently bypass the same validation MCP tools enforce.
implementation:
  primary: src/dsl/index.ts
  test_code: tests/dsl.test.ts
  test_code_status: manual
see_also:
  - usm/mcp-write
  - usm/schema-v1
  - usm/query-layer
